---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **순위가 시청을 얼마나 말해 주나.** (`/rank-tells`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **우리에게 불리한 수를 우리가 먼저 낸다.** 이 사이트가 파는 것이 순위인데,
 *    순위는 시청을 절반쯤밖에 못 말한다. 그것을 화면에 적는다.
 * ⛔ **한국 작품이 사는 차트에서 제일 적게 말해 준다**는 것을 숨기지 않는다.
 * ⛔ **세계 차트로 잰 것을 나라 차트에 옮기지 않는다.** 나라 차트엔 시청 칸이 0줄이다.
 * ⛔ 순위표를 안 만든다. 작품 이름을 안 낸다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-rank-tells.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-rank-tells.json';

const 몫 = (c) => data.narrowing.find((x) => x.chart === c).narrowedPc;
const 한국차트 = data.byChart.find((c) => c.chart === 'TV (Non-English)');
const 백만 = (n) => `${(n / 1e6).toFixed(1)}m`;

const TITLE = 'What a chart position does not tell you about how much was watched';
const DESC =
  `Netflix attaches hours viewed to its global lists and to none of its ${data.countryRowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')} `
  + `country rows. On the global lists, knowing a title's rank narrows the range of possible hours by `
  + `${몫('TV (Non-English)')}% on the non-English series chart — the one Korean titles sit on — leaving weeks at the `
  + `same rank still ${한국차트.withinRankSpread}× apart.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/rank-tells">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Every figure on this site is built from chart positions. This page is about what a position is
    worth as a measure of viewing — including the part where it is worth less than we would like.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Netflix gives hours to the world and none to the countries</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Netflix Top 10 lists</th><th>Rows</th><th>Rows carrying hours viewed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Per-country weekly lists</td>
            <td>{data.countryRowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.countryRowsWithViewing}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Global weekly lists</td>
            <td>{data.globalRowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.globalRowsWithViewing.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{data.countryRowsWithViewing} of {data.countryRowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}.</b> Not a
      sample, not a lag — Netflix simply does not publish how many hours a country watched anything.
      Every country figure on this site therefore rests on a position, and the question is how much a
      position carries.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How much a rank narrows the range</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Chart</th><th>Spread knowing nothing</th><th>Spread knowing the rank</th><th>Narrowed by</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byChart.map((c) => (
            <tr class={c.chart === 'TV (Non-English)' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{c.chart}</td>
              <td>{c.allSpread}×</td>
              <td>{c.withinRankSpread}×</td>
              <td class="tot">{몫(c.chart)}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.whyChartFixed}</p>
    <p class="warn">
      🔴 <b>The row that matters to us is the highlighted one.</b> Korean series appear on the
      non-English television chart, and that is where a rank narrows the range <b>least</b> —
      {' '}{몫('TV (Non-English)')}%, against {몫('Films (English)')}% on the English film chart. Two weeks
      at the same rank on that chart are still <b>{한국차트.withinRankSpread}× apart</b> in hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a rank does carry</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Chart</th><th>Median hours at rank 1</th><th>At rank 10</th><th>Ratio</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byChart.map((c) => (
            <tr class={c.chart === 'TV (Non-English)' ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{c.chart}</td>
              <td>{백만(c.medianTop)}</td>
              <td>{백만(c.medianTenth)}</td>
              <td class="tot">{c.topOverTenth}×</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      A position is not nothing. Across every chart, a typical week at rank 1 is six to eight times
      the hours of a typical week at rank 10, and the ordering never inverts. <b>Rank tells you where
      a title sits against the other nine. It does not tell you the size of the audience</b>, and on
      the chart Korean titles live on it tells you that least well of the four.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rank by rank, on the chart Korean titles sit on</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Weeks</th><th>Median hours</th><th>Spread within this rank</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {한국차트.byRank.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{r.rank}</td>
              <td>{r.weeks}</td>
              <td>{백만(r.medianHours)}</td>
              <td class="tot">{r.spread}×</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Read the last column down. Even at rank 1 — the position with the least room above it — a high
      week is several times a low week. The number in the third column is a median, not a value: any
      single week can sit well away from it.
    </p>
  </section>

  {data.addingWeeks && (
    <section>
      <h2>What one more column buys — and the trap in measuring it</h2>
      <p class="note lead-in">
        If a rank alone leaves this much unsaid, the next question is what to put beside it. We tested
        two candidates: Netflix's own <b>weeks in top 10</b>, and <b>how many countries' charts the
        title was on that week</b> — a column Netflix does not publish anywhere and we build from the
        {' '}{data.countryRowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')} country rows.
      </p>
      <p class="warn">
        <b>The trap first.</b> {data.whySham} Every figure in the table below has that sham figure
        subtracted from it.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Chart</th><th>Rank alone</th><th>+ weeks in top 10</th><th>+ countries that week</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {data.addingWeeks.map((x) => (
              <tr class={x.chart === 'TV (Non-English)' ? 'edge' : ''}>
                <td class="nm">{x.chart}</td>
                <td>{몫(x.chart)}%</td>
                <td>{x.realGainPc > 0 ? `+${x.realGainPc}` : x.realGainPc}pp</td>
                <td class="tot">{x.breadthRealGainPc > 0 ? `+${x.breadthRealGainPc}` : x.breadthRealGainPc}pp</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        🔴 <b>On the chart Korean titles sit on, Netflix's own column is worth nothing</b> —
        {' '}{data.addingWeeks.find((x) => x.chart === 'TV (Non-English)').realGainPc} points, which is
        to say less than a meaningless label. The column we build ourselves is worth
        {' '}{data.addingWeeks.find((x) => x.chart === 'TV (Non-English)').breadthRealGainPc} points,
        taking the chart from {몫('TV (Non-English)')}% to
        {' '}{data.addingWeeks.find((x) => x.chart === 'TV (Non-English)').breadthNarrowedPc}%.
      </p>
      <p class="note fine">
        Had we picked without measuring, we would have picked weeks in top 10 — it arrives in the file
        already and costs nothing to pass on. It is the one that does not work.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/rank-tells" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>🔴 Why this cannot be carried to the country charts.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Spread is a ratio of percentiles, not a standard deviation.</b> Hours viewed have a long
        upper tail, and a mean would sit above most of the weeks it describes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{data.weeksSpanned} weeks</b> of global lists are behind every figure here.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/world-share">What share of chart places Korean titles hold</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/rank-shape">Where on the chart they sit</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
